{
  "reasoning": "The lecture moves from Unity’s built-in support to scripting support, then to deployment, and finally to SDK-based extension using Vuforia. I grouped the opening and scripting-setup material together because the first practical step is understanding why scripting is used and how a C# script is created and attached. I then cut at clear bridges where the lecturer introduces the next concept: script file anatomy, functions/events, prefab instantiation, control-flow constructs, Transform, coroutines, InvokeRepeating, input support, Unity build settings, and SDK need. The Vuforia sample-project steps from sentence 191 through 263 were kept as one practical implementation sub-goal so that the scene, player, and deployment settings remain self-contained. The final segment is the closing summary.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 1,
      "subgoal": "Explain why Unity scripting support is needed and how to set up and create a C# script that customizes game-object behavior."
    },
    {
      "start": 30,
      "subgoal": "Describe the basic anatomy of a Unity C# script and how to inspect variable values and console output."
    },
    {
      "start": 58,
      "subgoal": "Explain how Unity scripts use built-in functions such as Destroy, user-defined functions, and event functions to control behavior and respond to gameplay events."
    },
    {
      "start": 88,
      "subgoal": "Describe how to instantiate prefabs at runtime by creating a script, attaching it to a game object, and referencing a prefab."
    },
    {
      "start": 99,
      "subgoal": "Describe the conditional and loop constructs supported in Unity C# scripts, including for, while, do-while, and foreach loops."
    },
    {
      "start": 115,
      "subgoal": "Explain how the Transform property stores and manipulates a game object’s position, rotation, and scale."
    },
    {
      "start": 120,
      "subgoal": "Explain how coroutines spread tasks across multiple frames, why they are not threads, and how they make effects such as fading visible."
    },
    {
      "start": 139,
      "subgoal": "Describe how InvokeRepeating invokes a method after an initial delay and then repeatedly at fixed intervals."
    },
    {
      "start": 150,
      "subgoal": "Describe how Unity scripting supports user interaction with input devices and how SDKs can extend device-specific input support."
    },
    {
      "start": 154,
      "subgoal": "Describe how to use Unity build settings to compile and deploy an XR system to a selected platform."
    },
    {
      "start": 168,
      "subgoal": "Explain why platform-specific SDKs are needed and outline the layered architecture and general steps for integrating an SDK into Unity."
    },
    {
      "start": 191,
      "subgoal": "Set up, configure, and deploy a Vuforia marker-based AR sample project on Android, including license, database, scene, player settings, and build steps."
    },
    {
      "start": 264,
      "subgoal": "Explain how the Vuforia sample project recognizes a target image and displays a 3D model, and how similar SDKs can extend Unity’s functionality."
    },
    {
      "start": 279,
      "subgoal": "Summarize the main points of the lecture and close the session."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "reasoning": "For segment 4, I combined prefab instantiation with the following conditional and loop discussion because prefab instantiation alone was too short, and both are standard Unity scripting constructs for controlling behavior. For segment 6, I merged the Transform property with the following coroutine and InvokeRepeating material so that the segment forms one complete object-behavior-over-time sub-goal. For segment 8, I moved InvokeRepeating into that same object-behavior segment instead of leaving it as a tiny standalone topic. For segment 9, I merged input support with the deployment section so the segment becomes a practical complete-and-deploy-the-system step rather than a short isolated note. For segment 13, I did not keep the Vuforia outcome and generalization separate; I merged it into the Vuforia sample segment and started that segment at sentence 191 so the Vuforia case study is self-contained, while the earlier SDK rationale remains a separate segment.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 1,
      "subgoal": "Explain why Unity scripting support is needed for customized XR experiences and how to set up a C# scripting environment to create and attach scripts."
    },
    {
      "start": 30,
      "subgoal": "Describe the basic structure of a Unity C# script and how to inspect variable values and console output."
    },
    {
      "start": 58,
      "subgoal": "Explain how Unity scripts use built-in, user-defined, and event functions to control objects and respond to gameplay events."
    },
    {
      "start": 88,
      "subgoal": "Describe how to instantiate prefabs at runtime and use conditional and loop constructs to control script behavior."
    },
    {
      "start": 115,
      "subgoal": "Explain how the Transform property, coroutines, and InvokeRepeating enable timed or frame-based changes to game objects."
    },
    {
      "start": 150,
      "subgoal": "Describe how Unity scripting supports user input and how to build and deploy the system from the Unity editor."
    },
    {
      "start": 168,
      "subgoal": "Explain why external SDKs are needed, how they fit into Unity's layered architecture, and the general steps for integrating a platform-specific SDK."
    },
    {
      "start": 191,
      "subgoal": "Set up, configure, build, and demonstrate a Vuforia marker-based AR sample project that recognizes an image target and displays a 3D model."
    },
    {
      "start": 279,
      "subgoal": "Summarize the main points of the lecture and conclude the session."
    }
  ]
}
